 "Reference article", "slug": "orb"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q461585", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Globus_cruciger"], "tags": ["symbol", "Travel Writing & Geography", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "orb contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The orb's role as a symbol of divine authority significantly shaped the political landscape in medieval Europe, influencing monarchies and their claims to power well into modern times.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-03T05:56:02Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28641270022", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Globular object sometimes topped with a cross; christian symbol of authority", "newValue": "The orb, a globular object traditionally adorned with a cross, serves as a potent symbol of authority within Christian iconography, particularly during the Classical era (circa 500 BCE - 476 CE). This emblem, often associated with sovereign power, was frequently used by monarchs and church leaders across Europe and beyond, signifying their divine right to rule. Notable figures such as Charlemagne and later monarchs of the Holy Roman Empire adopted the orb in their regalia, intertwining political power with religious legitimacy.

